## P18-V01 POP in-flight callback after assignment removal — 2026-10-22

Reviewed the pinned Java 5.5.1 POP callback and consume-request path. Java
continues a `FOUND` callback that was already issued when rebalance removes the
POP queue: it updates pull statistics, filters messages, ACKs filtered
messages, and submits accepted messages. The consume request checks the
dropped flag before listener dispatch. Rust previously aborted the POP task
when removing the queue, discarding that response.

Rust now removes the worker from the assignment map and retires its task while
the issued response finishes. The POP loop observes the dropped queue before
issuing another request. Shutdown and destroy also drain retired task handles.
The new scripted regression removes the queue before releasing a FOUND reply
with a filtered message and valid receipt, then verifies the Broker receives
the filtered-message ACK and the retired worker exits.

Verification:

- `cargo test --offline -p rocketmq-classic-consumer --lib removed_pop_queue_finishes_inflight_found_callback_before_retiring --locked --quiet` — PASS.
- `cargo test --offline -p rocketmq-classic-consumer --lib --locked --quiet` — PASS, 257 tests.
- `cargo test --offline --workspace --all-features --lib --locked --quiet` — PASS; nonempty suites: 27, 257, 215, 154, 160 and 140 tests.
- `cargo clippy --offline --workspace --all-targets --all-features --locked -- -D warnings` — PASS.
- `cargo fmt --all -- --check` and `git diff --check` — PASS.

No Java fixture changed. This closes the tested in-flight callback behavior for
this assignment-removal case only. Broad P18-V01 source/API/configuration/flow
and reverse-export reconciliation, current-revision remote Ubuntu CI, native
Windows execution, and Phase 19 Broker/Docker evidence remain open. Protected
Phase 19 worktree paths were left untouched.
